BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Cartel and Other Cover Stories Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260910T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260910T190000
UID:bm-6215920260910@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/2026_09_10.png?cb=1787858697
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books\, in part
 nership with Freehand Books\, to celebrate the launch of\nThe Cartel and O
 ther Cover Stories\nby Wayne Grady.\nOrder a copy of\nThe Cartel\nhere.\nA
 bout the Book\nFrom bestselling novelist Wayne Grady comes the delightful 
 and inventive\nThe Cartel and Other Cover Stories\n\, his first collection
  of short fiction.\nJust as cover songs are both tributes to other musicia
 ns and original works in themselves\, here Grady masterfully reimagines cl
 assic short stories in a way that honours the originals while creating som
 ething entirely new.\n\nEach story in this collection takes its inspiratio
 n from works by international masters of the form\, including Anton Chekho
 v\, Franz Kafka\, and Lydia Davis\, drawing together new contexts and conn
 ections.\nFor instance\, in “La Gordita\,” inspired by Guy de Maupassa
 nt’s “Boule de Suif” about the French bourgeoisie\, Grady skewers we
 althy expats in Mexico as they make arrangements to leave their winter hom
 es when a drug cartel threatens to seize control of their town.\nBringing 
 together delectably stylish prose\, striking characters\, a foreword from 
 Margaret Atwood\, and “literary liner notes” that bring context to the
  original stories and the short story form\, Wayne Grady’s collection of
  cover stories is not to be missed!\nAbout the Author\nWayne Grady is the 
 author of more than a dozen works of nonfiction and three novels\, includi
 ng\nEmancipation Day\n\, which won the Amazon First Novel Award and was lo
 nglisted for the Giller Prize. Wayne is also an award-winning literary tra
 nslator\, and won the Governor-General’s Award for his translation of An
 tonine Maillet’s On the Eighth Day. He divides his time between Kingston
 \, Ontario\, and San Miguel de Allende\, Mexico\, with his wife\, the nove
 list and biographer Merilyn Simonds. For more about Wayne and his work\, v
 isit waynegrady.ca.
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:London Curious Wanderers Author Talk
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T190000
UID:bm-6200720260916@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/2026_09_15.png?cb=1787763727
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books as we hos
 t Jack Chesher\, the author of the\nLondon Curious Wanderers\nseries.\nLon
 don: A Guide for Curious Wanderers\nLondon: Hidden Corners for Curious Wan
 derers\nAbout the Books\nLondon is famous for its museums\, each one full 
 of treasures and relics – but the biggest museum in the capital is the c
 ity itself.\nFrom the stories behind unusual street names\, to the trees i
 n our parks\; railings made from recycled WWII stretchers\, to shrapnel da
 mage on walls\; the hidden symbols on post boxes\, to prehistoric tree tru
 nks – there is a rich history hidden in the oft-overlooked details of th
 e city's streets\, gardens\, parks and buildings.\nThis richly detailed an
 d beautifully illustrated book provides a miscellany of historic features 
 and curiosities to spot as you wander around the capital. Whether you’ve
  always wondered why there are cattle troughs on your route to work\, why 
 bollards often look like upside down cannons or wanted to know what a Vict
 orian stink pipe is – this book will provide the tools to decipher Londo
 n’s secret code\, and introduce you to a treasure trove of hidden spots 
 to explore.\nThe book comes complete with maps so you can spot these detai
 ls yourself on walks through the capital.\n\nSo\, pop on a sturdy pair of 
 shoes and get ready to turn the city into the museum you never knew you ha
 d.\nAbout the Author\nJack Chesher grew up in Essex\, and exciting day tri
 ps to London have always been part of his life. After graduating from Bris
 tol University\, where he studied history\, he finally took the plunge and
  moved to the capital in July 2020. A passion for walking and history insp
 ired him to launch @LivingLondonHistory in September 2020\, which currentl
 y has over 578k followers. Documenting his journey to discovering the city
 ’s fascinating and vibrant past by exploring its streets\, he believes L
 ondon itself is the greatest museum of all.
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
